[THOUGHT] Absence of mechanical symptoms (clicking, locking, giving way) makes meniscal or ligamentous pathology less likely. Combined with non-traumatic onset, no trauma history, and non-inflammatory synovial fluid, the picture points away from structural injury or acute inflammation. Remaining possibilities include:  
- Early or atypical inflammatory arthritis (e.g., seronegative spondyloarthropathy), though fluid is non-inflammatory;  
- Referred pain (e.g., lumbar radiculopathy — L3/L4 nerve root irritation can refer to medial knee);  
- Patellofemoral pain syndrome (often insidious, but can flare acutely);  
- Stress-related or overuse phenomenon;  
- Less likely: malignancy, neuropathic pain, or systemic illness (e.g., Lyme, sarcoid).  

To assess for referred pain, lumbar spine evaluation is reasonable — but “Lumbar Spine X-ray” or “MRI” would be low-yield without back pain or neurologic symptoms. Instead, a simple screening test: check for patellar tenderness or crepitus via physical exam. Since “Physical Examination - Extremity Inspection and Palpation” is available and covers inspection for swelling/erythema and palpation for focal tenderness (e.g., medial joint line, patella, pes anserinus), it’s the most appropriate next step to localize pain and identify reproducible tenderness — which would support a diagnosis like pes anserinus bursitis (consistent with ultrasound finding of fluid deep to pes anserinus tendons).

[TEST] Physical Examination - Extremity Inspection and Palpation

